      text: 'Explain how the structure of a cell membrane allows it to regulate what enters and exits the cell.',
      topic: 'Cell Biology',
      modelAnswer: 'The cell membrane is a phospholipid bilayer with the hydrophilic (water-loving) heads facing outward (toward water) and the hydrophobic (water-fearing) tails facing inward, away from water. This arrangement creates a selectively permeable barrier. Small, nonpolar molecules (like O₂ and CO₂) can diffuse directly through the lipid bilayer. Larger or charged molecules (like glucose, ions, and proteins) require protein channels or carrier proteins embedded in the membrane to cross. Some proteins act as pumps that use ATP (active transport) to move substances against their concentration gradient. This selective control over what enters and exits allows the cell to maintain homeostasis by keeping internal conditions stable.',
