      diveDeep: 'The fluid mosaic model (Singer and Nicolson, 1972) describes the cell membrane as a dynamic, two-dimensional fluid with proteins "floating" in a lipid bilayer. Membrane proteins serve diverse functions: channels (facilitate diffusion), carriers (conformational change transports molecules), pumps (active transport using ATP), receptors (signal transduction), and glycoproteins (cell recognition). Cholesterol molecules inserted between phospholipid tails regulate membrane fluidity — increasing fluidity at low temperatures and decreasing it at high temperatures. This temperature regulation of fluidity is important for organisms living in environments with variable temperatures.',
