      text: 'A student claims that removing wolves from Yellowstone National Park would benefit the ecosystem by protecting elk populations. Evaluate this claim using your knowledge of food webs and ecosystem stability.',
      topic: 'Ecology',
      modelAnswer: 'The student\'s claim is incorrect. Wolves are a keystone predator in Yellowstone — their presence regulates elk populations. Without wolves, elk populations would increase dramatically, overgrazing vegetation such as willows and aspens along riverbanks. This loss of vegetation would cause riverbank erosion, destroy beaver habitat (beavers need willows), and reduce songbird populations that nest in willows. The "trophic cascade" initiated by wolf removal would destabilize the entire ecosystem. When wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone in 1995, vegetation recovered, rivers became more stable, and biodiversity increased — demonstrating that removing a keystone predator does not benefit the ecosystem but instead destabilizes it.',
